  "worked_solution": "Let $f(x)=\\frac{2}{x}+x.$ Let's first calculate the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2}{x}+x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2}{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{-1})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =-2{x}^{-2}+1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at our point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={f}^{\u2032}(2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{2}^{2}}+1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{4}+1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the tangent is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-2) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x-1+3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the tangent line intercepts the $y$-axis, we set $x=0$ and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}(0)+2 \\\\ y & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(0,2).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
